<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan van Ruusbroec</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1293">1293</a>–<a href="/years/1381">1381</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John of Ruusbroec</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan van Ruusbroec was a Brabantine mystic and priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> who withdrew in 1343 to Groenendaal in the Sonian Forest, where he became prior of a community of canons regular and died in 1381. His Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> works, above all The Spiritual Espousals, describe the stages of the soul's union with God. He influenced Geert Groote and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devotio-moderna">Devotio Moderna</a> and was beatified in 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-van-ruusbroec.jpg" alt="Jan van Ruusbroec" width="276" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Polylerus at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Van_Ruysbroeck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
